# AsphaltAi

AI-assisted pothole reporting for Bengaluru roads. Upload a road photo, a pretrained YOLO model finds the potholes, and the report (location, pothole count and a severity estimate) is saved and shown on an interactive map.

> **Status:** working prototype. Evaluation on a full labeled test set is still in progress, so **no accuracy figures are claimed yet**.

![Detection page](docs/detect.png)
![Dashboard map](docs/dashboard.png)

## What it does

- Upload a JPG or PNG road photo and see a box and a confidence score for every detected pothole
- Detections the model is at least 50% sure about are counted as **confirmed**. Weaker ones are listed in a table but not counted, and can be drawn on the photo with a checkbox
- **Rule-based severity estimate** (Low, Medium, High or Unclear) with a plain-English explanation of why
- Reads the GPS location from the photo when it has one and suggests the locality from it (free OpenStreetMap lookup). Otherwise the location is typed in
- A report needs a locality and coordinates inside Bengaluru before it can be saved, so reports with a missing or impossible location are refused
- Saves reports to a local SQLite database
- **Dashboard:** map of reports coloured by severity, a severity filter, and a list of areas with repeated reports
- Comes with fake sample reports pre-loaded so the dashboard works straight away
- `evaluate.py` measures the model on photos labeled by a human (see below)

## Tech stack

Python, Streamlit, Ultralytics YOLO (PyTorch), Pillow, SQLite, Folium (OpenStreetMap), pandas

## Run it locally (Windows)

Python 3.10 to 3.12 is recommended, because PyTorch can lag behind the newest Python versions.

```
python -m venv venv
venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
streamlit run app.py
```

On first use of the Detect page, the app downloads a free pretrained pothole model from Hugging Face (`Samdutse/pothole-yolov8`). No account or API key is needed. If the download fails, download `best.pt` from that model's page and place it at `models/best.pt`. Check the model page for its license and training data before reusing it.

## How severity is estimated

Severity uses only **confirmed** detections (confidence 50% or more), and only two things: how much of the photo each pothole covers, and how many potholes there are.

| Level | When |
|---|---|
| High | One pothole covers 10% or more of the photo, or two or more potholes are each at least 3%, or four or more potholes |
| Medium | One pothole covers 3% or more, or there are two or more potholes |
| Low | A single small pothole |
| Unclear | No detection reaches 50% confidence, so a person should check the photo |

Confidence is deliberately **not** used for severity. A pothole the model is 95% sure about can still be small. Confidence only decides which detections count and how reliable the estimate is.

The thresholds (3%, 10%, 4 potholes) are starting values and have **not** been validated against human ratings yet.

## Evaluating the model

`evaluate.py` compares the model with boxes a human drew on test photos and reports precision, recall, F1, AP50, how often the model is right at each confidence level, results per photo type (rain, dark, multiple potholes, no potholes), and speed at different image sizes. It can also save review pictures that show the human boxes and the model boxes together.

```
python evaluate.py --selftest        check the maths without any photos
python evaluate.py                   run the evaluation
python evaluate.py --draft-labels    let the model draft labels for a human to correct
```

Test photos go in `test_data/images` and YOLO-format labels in `test_data/labels`. Name photos with their type first, for example `rain_01.jpg`.

