## Known limitations

- **Locations are self-reported.** Anyone can type any coordinates, and photo GPS can be edited, so reported locations are not verified.
- **The locality suggestion needs internet.** Only the coordinates (never the photo) are sent to OpenStreetMap's free Nominatim service, which has a usage policy that rules out heavy traffic.
- **The model can mistake patched or repaired road for a pothole.** This showed up in my own testing, so patched roads should get their own group in the evaluation.
- **Severity comes from how large the pothole looks in the photo.** A close-up makes any pothole look bigger. A flat photo cannot show depth, so depth is never claimed. Road damage around the pothole is not considered.
- **The model is pretrained, not trained by me.** My own work is the app, the severity rules, and the evaluation.
- **Sample reports on the dashboard are fake**, added so the demo works from the start.
- Reports are stored in a local SQLite file, so they do not survive on a typical cloud deployment. Persistent storage is planned.

## Project structure

```
app.py             the Streamlit web app
evaluate.py        evaluation script
clean_reports.py   removes saved reports with a missing or out-of-area location
requirements.txt   Python packages
```